  "upload": {
    "title": "Your iPhone Fires 30,000 Dots at Your Face 👀 #shorts",
    "description": "Face ID's TrueDepth camera projects more than 30,000 invisible infrared dots to build a depth map of your face, which the Secure Enclave compares with your enrolled face. That depth data is why a flat photo can't unlock the phone, and Apple puts the chance that a random person unlocks your iPhone at less than 1 in 1,000,000. The twist: Apple says the probability is higher for twins and siblings who look like you (and children under 13), and recommends using a passcode if you're concerned. Some identical twins have unlocked each other's phones in tests.",
